# Normalize line endings in the Git repo to LF.
#
# - `text=auto` lets git decide binary-vs-text per file (binary stays untouched).
# - `eol=lf` stores every text file as LF AND checks it out as LF on every platform
#   (it overrides core.autocrlf), so the working tree is LF on Windows too and the
#   repo stays consistent across macOS / Linux / Windows / CI.
#
# What still produces CRLF on Windows is tools writing files after checkout: Python's
# text mode (open files with newline=LF), editors without an LF setting, and clones made with the
# Git-for-Windows default core.autocrlf=true (set core.autocrlf=false globally, or
# ship a global attributes file with `* text=auto eol=lf`).
# Keep the VS Code files.eol setting at LF (see .vscode/settings.json) so saves write LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never normalize binary assets (would corrupt them). The `* text=auto` above
# already covers them; this is belt-and-braces for files with no obvious ext.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xlsm binary
*.docx binary
*.zip binary
*.tar binary
*.gz binary
*.bin binary
*.pyc binary
